{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786476531,"nonce":"lwAsqcGi+uWRIs2QmPdVZ+bJbvw=","message":"Fixed in 795304e, migration 00040. The uniqueness tuple now carries the rule key; the fingerprint stays content-only so a renamed or moved rule is still recognisable as the same text. keyPredicate is lenient on BOTH sides, so the promotion reports drift symmetrically. On the judgement call: TestTwoRulesWithIdenticalContentCollapseIntoOneRow was a defect, not de-duplication -- a row is the definition PLUS the key it was captured under, one row cannot carry two keys, and every read path filters on rule_name, so the loser's capture is permanently unreachable. De-duplication the reader cannot undo is loss. Genuine de-duplication (same rule, every fire) is untouched.","files":null}]}